### Coordinator checkpoint after review round 3: authorize another bounded round

Decision `ead1d705` by coordinator `local_bf62b311-1fa1-4cfd-8ce2-974226c01d3c` of Feature #1961 for #1962. Authority: the orchestrate-feature invocation, quoted verbatim from the run record: "C:/Program Files/Git/orchestrate-feature #1961"

- Round state: round 3 is complete (limit 3 before this decision); its planned reviewers (boundary, undirected) exited, and every report has its adjudication (6222795139 in 6222927525, 6223054405 in 6223147680, 6223383016 in 6223516563, 6223680529 in 6223816400, 6224347210 in 6224554059, 6224851543 in 6224992329).
- Independently reviewed through `2fb944aa7a0add9f272720f73ba3c1909327ddf0`.
- Head `3626459970eba05554ed39a5c677fb570ba585ef`. Unreviewed: `2fb944aa7..362645997`, 1 commit (`362645997` fix(github): refuse curl globs, GraphQL ticket reads and OR searches outright (#1962)), diff SHA-256 `7358f4f67cb1530b1dd2f58febc78c45854f97ec073fec1ee31e271abd44c281`.
- CI on the head: changes / detect passed, linked-issue passed, linked-issue passed, linked-issue passed, changes / detect passed, changes / detect passed, changes / detect passed, notifier passed, ${{ matrix.package }}${{ (((matrix.shards > 1) && format(' ({0}/{1})', matrix.shard, matrix.shards)) || '') }} passed, driver-changes / detect passed, tsc passed, notifier-tests passed, skills-checks (ubuntu-latest) passed, test-suite passed, typecheck passed, skills-checks (windows-latest) passed, driver-checks (ubuntu-latest) passed, driver-checks (windows-latest) pending, vitest-api-integration (ubuntu-latest, 2.1.9) passed, vitest-api-integration (ubuntu-latest, 3.2.6) passed, vitest-api-integration (windows-latest, 2.1.9) pending, vitest-api-integration (windows-latest, 3.2.6) pending.
- Findings across all rounds: 30 accepted; unresolved: none.
- Shared mechanisms: All 30 accepted findings across three rounds are one mechanism: the guard reads a command's text statically and each report found a place where that reading disagreed with what gh (pflag precedence, attached values, string slices, aliases), curl (option clusters, globs, userinfo, ports, fragments), bash and PowerShell (environment inheritance), or GitHub (REST path canonicalization, GraphQL traversals from any entry point, boolean search semantics) actually do. Every one is in the switch-on position only; the off position, which is what merges and runs until #1967, was compared against origin/main by three reviewers (1152, 22 and 832 sampled decisions) with no difference, and the corrections are reached only under repos.cutover or are explicitly split on it. From round 2 the corrections stopped modelling the tools and replaced each class with a fail-closed rule (any mention of the code repository refuses; every whole API URL is a destination; unknown GraphQL entry points refuse), and the round 3 undirected correction at 3626459970 goes furthest: a curl argument naming GitHub with a glob character is refused outright, any GraphQL selection of issues, comments, reviews, discussions or timelines is refused whatever repository it names, a search with OR is unscoped, and every curl destination is checked for internal typed creation. These prevent the class rather than patch instances, at the cost of disclosed over-refusals.
- Defects introduced by corrections: Corrections introduced at least three defects in earlier rounds, all found by the next reviewer: round 1's 'value of the preceding flag' exclusion produced the -XGET destination leak; round 1's 'a quoted word with a space is not a flag' rule hid --body=<url> selectors; round 3's -g detection and range-first-value glob expansion produced round 3 undirected findings 1 and 6. Each regression came from modelling a tool's syntax; the final commit removes those models. That history is exactly why the final correction, which again changes switch-on behaviour and has had no independent review, should be read once more rather than accepted on the writer's verification alone. Stability of the off position has never been in question.
- Broader or simpler repair: The broader alternative is allow-lists: only enumerated gh subcommands and REST paths on the code repository and no GraphQL at all. It would close the long tail of exotic forms but risks refusing commands #1963 to #1965 need at cutover, and the ticket scopes the hook as a guardrail against forgetting the type or the repository, not against evasion. The writer's fail-closed rules already cover every form an agent retargeting a command by mistake would produce; most of round 3's findings (cross-owner GraphQL traversal, host globs, attached-value tricks, OR searches) are evasion-grade. I agree with keeping the current rules and not moving to allow-lists. One simplification is worth the round 4 reviewer's attention: the new glob refusal exempts arguments holding a quote or a space as bodies, while HTTP_URL also excludes quotes, so a GitHub URL glob argument that carries a quoted query string may be neither refused as a glob nor read as a destination; if curl sends it and GitHub answers, the simpler rule is to drop the body exemption for any argument that starts with a GitHub URL.
- Residual risk: 1. Switch off, which merges and runs until #1967: none found by any reviewer; decisions match origin/main and the committed repos.json was never flipped. 2. Switch on: the final correction at 3626459970 is unreviewed and behaviour-changing; given three correction-introduced defects earlier, a further leak or over-refusal in its four blanket rules is plausible (the quoted-query glob gap above is one candidate). Beyond that, forms outside the static scope the PR discloses (variables, earlier exports, query files, curl -K, other clients, aliases, wrappers) remain by design. 3. Over-refusals accepted on the fail-closed side are listed in the PR's Notes; the one most likely to bite at cutover is that GraphQL ticket reads on the internal repository are refused, so #1963 to #1965 must use gh issue -R or REST, and no current skill text uses gh api graphql.
- Verification coverage: node --test .claude/hooks/typed-issue-hook.test.mjs passes at 3626459970 with 1326 checks: the original tables under an explicit off config, wired fixture checkouts in both positions and with a malformed repos file, and a switch-on table of refusals and allowances run through the real hook script. Every correction's new rows were shown to fail against the previous reviewed head's hook by snapshot and restore verified with cmp. The shared-stash, policy, portability and local-test hook tests pass. Hook Tests CI passed in full on da29d919f and 2fb944aa7; on 3626459970 the Test Suite gate, linked-issue, notifier-tests, tsc and change detection have passed and the Windows hook-tests, skills and driver jobs were still running when this was decided. Three reviewers compared the off position against origin/main (1152, 22 and 832 decisions) with no difference. Each report's probes ran only in private clones through the reserved runner.
- What another round would establish: One more bounded round on 3626459970 establishes whether the four blanket rules are sound: no new leak (in particular whether a GitHub URL glob argument with a quoted query string escapes both the glob refusal and the destination check) and no over-refusal of a command the workflow runs at cutover. The change since 2fb944aa7 deletes about 100 lines of modelling and adds four short checks, so a single undirected reviewer at the default route covers it. Round 4 is the first review of a repair-by-simplification rather than another sweep of instances, which is where this review converges or does not. Evasion-grade findings it may still surface are adjudicated against the ticket's guardrail scope: recorded as disclosed static limits in the PR's Notes unless the fix is itself a simplification, and not chased further.
- Agreed behavior preserved; architecture unchanged.
- Decision: The merge gate admits only the last independently reviewed head (2fb944aa7) or a head whose unreviewed corrections this record accepted. 2fb944aa7 carries six accepted, unfixed findings, so it cannot merge, and accepting 3626459970 unreviewed is refused by this run's own history: the defect class has not yet been shown to stop recurring under the new rules, three earlier corrections introduced defects, and CI on the head is still pending. The writer's final commit is the repair the checkpoint guidance calls for when one mechanism keeps producing findings (remove the mechanism's modelling and fail closed), and the guidance then calls for a bounded round on the repair. So the decision is a one-round extension, limit 4, with the reviewer composition left to the writer's review-pr defaults and a single undirected reviewer recommended, and with the adjudication rule above so the round converges instead of restarting the sweep. The pre-existing switch-off gap the writer raised (gh issue new, gh's alias of create, is not typed-checked with the switch off, exactly as on the base) stays as it is: the settled decision on #1961 keeps the off position identical to today, and the on position already checks it; it is disclosed in the PR's Notes. Next: The writer launches round 4 on 3626459970 with reviewRoundLimit 4 and extendedReviewAuthorization set to the invocation quoted in the published decision, one undirected reviewer at the repository's reviewer defaults; adjudicates evasion-grade findings against the ticket's guardrail scope as disclosed limits unless the fix is a simplification; and reports the next checkpoint after round 4's verified fixes. The PR stays draft until then. The gh issue new off-position gap stays as disclosed.
- Round 4 may launch with `reviewRoundLimit` 4 and `extendedReviewAuthorization` set to the invocation quoted above, verbatim. Limit reason: The unreviewed change since 2fb944aa7 is one commit that replaces tool modelling with four blanket fail-closed rules; one bounded round with a single undirected reviewer covers it, and a further round would restart the sweep of evasion-grade instances the ticket scopes out. The next checkpoint follows round 4's verified fixes, and the count never resets.

